  "notes": "Memphis-soul harmony wearing a crunk-era drum kit, and the research states the contrast itself: \"genuine changes rather than crunk's static i\". So this entry carries the widest harmony in the batch — ii–V–I and I–vi–IV–V four-bar loops with sevenths and ninths at three quarters of the extension weight, and a `keys` list that is half major. Straight time, backbeat on 2&4, kick on 1 and the \"&\" of 2 exactly as written, ghost 16ths on the snare, tambourine and handclaps as a standing layer, and the 808 pushed down to \"weight only\". ⛔ `drums.bass808.role` therefore moves off `bassline`: he is a bassist (son of the Bar-Kays' James Alexander) and the research says played lines with syncopation and passing tones over an 808 underneath, so the `bassline` block grows a real part — `independent_riff`, `followRootsProb` 0.45, four passing tones and the highest `anticipationProb` here. `counter_riff` is the engine's only other 808 role and is a near-fit rather than a description; `slideProb` is held near zero so the sub sits still under the played line. Note density is high and `countermelody` is standard rather than optional, both stated. His spoken tag is modelled as the 1–2 bar intro the research calls it. ⚠ `medium`, tracking the research's high-medium: instrumentation, soul lineage, the tag convention and the credits are sourced, and the paragraph marks the tempo numbers themselves LOW-CONF. The 70–80 half-time club band is NOT modelled — this entry commits to the 82–100 straight centre. The perc lanes and the chord weights are INFERRED. ⚠ `fills.snareRollLadder` is off: with both fill periods at 4 bars the big fill wins and the inherited ladder reads no seed, so it would write one identical fill on all 200. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 50 to 49 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 50 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
